hey gq i am back this is the fahim  alexander philadelphia pennsylvania  home of the legendary philly beard today  i'm going to show you guys how to shape  up and maintain  curly textured beards  grooming curly textured bears requires  different  maintenance and grooming techniques to  shape up your curly beard  you would need a cool men's line shampoo  and conditioner  beard oil the blow dryer and comb  attachment  a powerful trimmer to shape up the beard  the razor  at the end to clean up the excess hair  off the cheeks  curly bears are harder to clean compared  to straight hair beards  curly beards hold more dirt and debris  step one shampoo and conditioning the  beard  when shopping for a shampoo make sure  you look for a product specifically  designed to shampoo  curly beards so what i'm using is the 12  men's line  shampoo which help gain moisture and  strengthen the hair  when shampooing the beard make sure you  use your fingertips  to exfoliate the beard and get  underneath the beard in the hair to get  to the skin  you're going to do this repeatedly until  you get all of the  access to breathe the oil and dirt from  the beard  and on the skin this will help minimize  ingrown hair and redness on the surface  of the skin  make sure you guys rinse well then onto  the conditioner  it's also important to use a conditioner  that's  great for curly beards make sure you use  a generous amount  of conditioner in the beard and let it  sit for 10 minutes  my favorite that i like to use is the 12  12  men's conditioner it softens the facial  hair without clogging the pores  now we're ready for step two fertilizing  the lawn  my lawn at home i like it to be green i  give my lawn at home its proper  nutrients so it can be  green so i compare that to my beard so  it can be nice  full thick and dark i'm going to give it  proper nutrients beard oil helps promote  natural growth helps prevent breakage in  ingrown hairs  it's very important to choose a beard  oil that's not too  thick because you don't want to clog  your pores it's definitely to choose a  beard oil that's not  overbearing far as the smell you don't  want to override your cologne  i usually use like four to six drops so  i can cover the whole lawn  exfoliating the beard getting the chin  getting the size getting underneath  preparing to pull that beard up  smells great the beard oil  adds an extra layer and moisture for  protection myself personally  i use my beard oil in the morning and  when i get home  after a shower i shampoo my beard i  apply  beard oil once again so i can sleep  through the night after shampooing the  conditioning  and rinsing the beard oil helps restore  the natural oils  in your beard it's very important to  keep your beard  moisturized because curly hair is easy  to break  and our next step will be blow dry step  three you want to pull out the beard  i know some of you guys out there  viewing this video that do not have a  blow dryer i  really recommend that you get one  blow dryers do not damage the hair  especially after you added the oil  as you can see using the blow dryer  makes your curly beard easy to work with  using this comb attachment helps pour  your beer out to its highest point  you definitely can blow dry your hair as  much as you want when it's damp  but it's very important learn your  temperatures  you want to keep your blow dry on a warm  temperature depending on how much hair  you have on your beard you want to turn  the heat up a little bit but not too hot  so you can burn yourself  the beard here is now pulled out and  relaxed ready to go into the next step  now that your beard is pulled out we're  ready for the shape up  step four lining it up  for this step you will need a well  adjusted trimmer  i'm going to use my caliber 22 stingers  what that means you want to have a well  calibrated  trimmer there's a lot of guys in the  barber community that are really good at  adjusting trimmers maybe you want to  contact one of them guys  to send your blaze off to get adjusted  it's very important  that you stay away from your natural  beard line  when shaping it up because you don't  want to cause  any irritation or any burns  so just stay low as possible i like to  start at the highest point  of my beard around the temple area then  i come down  gradually to a circular motion into  the mustache then i like to hit the  bottom of my mustache then on top  then i repeat the same process on the  other side  now it's very difficult to hit the sides  so you're kind of like if you have a  double mirror in your bathroom  you're going to use that and you're just  going to kind of like pour your hair to  the side  then you're going to go down cleaning  that excess hair up around your line  then down to your neck area clean that  excess hair off  until it's all cleaned off  it's an essential to have that double  mirror in your bathroom  to achieve those tight lines on your  curly beard  it's very important if you don't have  double mirrors to help prevent  irritation and bumps you want to stay  far away  from your natural line and just clean  underneath  in the back you kind of want to pour  your hair to the front  step 5 cut down the beard so after  that's done  i'm going to pick up my 44 fmj's which  stand for full metal jackets from  colliver  this clipper is very diverse and you can  use it on  any texture of hair a trimmer is  strictly used for  shaping up outlining and detailing of  the beard or the hairline  a clipper is strictly for cutting down  to your desired length on your beard or  on your hair  when trimming down the mustache you want  to use like a number one guard  fully extend the blade then just go  south with it  one guards are very very important i'm  going to trim down my mustache  i like to go freehand so i'm going to  start right below the temple  just even strokes down to the chin area  then i'm going to start on my other side  repeat the same  steps just making sure it's nice and  neat  now that my beard is all shaped up and  cleaned up and looking fresh and my  lines are tight  and even to the circular motion is  on to the last and final step step 6  finishing touches sometimes the trimmer  doesn't get the skin smooth enough so  you want to go behind it with a razor  so i like to use a razor to clean up  them extra loose hairs  this will allow you to get that close  shade while preventing  ingrown hairs and irritation  multi-blade razors are known to irritate  curly textured beards this model has  two blades that's made to minimize  contact  it's made for men with textured beards  and sensitive skin  it'll give you that close shave without  leading to ingrown hairs  [Music]  you guys have now graduated from your  beard course  make sure you follow these tips to keep  your textured beard  healthy and avoid irritation for more of  these great tips  or mel grooming you can follow me on my  instagram  father barber one
 

